course: dictionary
lesson: 4113
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Gladiator» first means a man in ancient Rome who fought other men or wild animals in an arena to entertain the crowds. Read the words around it to find the right meaning: «Gladiators fought in arenas to entertain crowds» is about the Romans.
explain: A word can also be used in a figurative way, which means a picture of something rather than its exact meaning. A gladiator can also be someone who fights hard in a contest or an argument. If a debate partner is said to be a gladiator, the word paints a picture of a fierce fighter, but nobody is really in an arena.
explain: To check your meaning, put it back into the sentence. Does «fought hard in the argument» still make sense? Then you have it. If not, try the other meaning, and look at the clues around the word.
fact: A gladiator was a man in ancient Rome who fought in an arena.
fact: Gladiators fought with swords, nets or tridents, and crowds came to watch them.
fact: Many gladiators were slaves or prisoners, but some were volunteers and became famous, like sports stars.
fact: Not every gladiator fight ended in death, and the thumbs-up or thumbs-down sign may be a modern guess.
fact: Figurative use: a person who fights hard in a contest or argument can be called a gladiator.
exercise: «Gladiators fought in arenas to entertain crowds.» What is a gladiator? | A Roman coin used for buying bread | A chariot driver who raced at the Circus Maximus | *A man in ancient Rome who fought to entertain crowds | A kind of Roman bath
exercise: «Gladiators fought in arenas to entertain crowds.» Find and copy the word that tells you where gladiators fought. | fights | crowds | swords | *arenas
exercise: Maya says, "Jo was a real gladiator in the debate, arguing hard for every point." What does gladiator mean here? | A person who trains animals at a zoo | A quiet person who never speaks | *Someone who fights hard in a contest or argument | A Roman soldier who guards a city wall
test: What was a gladiator's job in ancient Rome? | To sell bread in the market | To build the Colosseum | *To fight in an arena to entertain crowds | To drive the chariots in the Circus Maximus
test: A history book says «in the arena». What does this phrase mean? | *In the fighting space where gladiators fought | At home in bed | At the market stall | In a Roman bath
exam: Which statement is true, based on the sentences about gladiators? | *Not every gladiator fight ended in death | Gladiators never fought in the Colosseum | All gladiators were volunteers who chose to fight | Every gladiator fight ended in death
